    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p45-q5-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "If $y=12x^3-3x^4+11$, show that $y'=12x^2(3-x)$ and $y''=36x(2-x)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y'=12x^2(3-x)$ and $y''=36x(2-x)$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y'&=36x^2-12x^3\\\\y'&=12x^2(3-x)\\\\y''&=72x-36x^2\\\\y''&=36x(2-x)\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 23
      },
      {
        "id": "p45-q5-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "By solving $y'=0$, find the coordinates of any stationary points.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(0,11)$ and $(3,92)$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}12x^2(3-x)&=0\\\\x&=0\\text{ or }3\\\\y(0)&=11\\\\y(3)&=12(3)^3-3(3)^4+11\\\\y(3)&=92\\end{align*}$$\nThe stationary points are $(0,11)$ and $(3,92)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 29
      },
      {
        "id": "p45-q5-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "By examining the sign of $y''$, establish the nature of the stationary point at $x=3$. Why does this method fail for the stationary point at $x=0$?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Maximum at $(3,92)$. At $x=0$, $y''=0$, so the second-derivative test is inconclusive.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y''(3)&=36(3)(2-3)\\\\y''(3)&=-108<0\\\\y''(0)&=0\\end{align*}$$\nThe stationary point $(3,92)$ is a maximum because the curve is concave down there. At $x=0$, the second-derivative test is inconclusive because $y''(0)=0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 21
      },
      {
        "id": "p45-q5-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Use a table of test values of $y'$ to show that there is a stationary inflection at $x=0$.",
