      {
        "id": "p45-q5-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "Show that there is a change in concavity at $x=2$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Concave up for $0<x<2$ and concave down for $x>2$; $(2,59)$ is a point of inflection.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y''&=36x(2-x)\\\\y''(2)&=0\\\\y(2)&=12(2)^3-3(2)^4+11\\\\y(2)&=59\\end{align*}$$\nFor $0<x<2$, both factors $x$ and $2-x$ are positive, so $y''>0$. For $x>2$, $x>0$ and $2-x<0$, so $y''<0$. Concavity changes from up to down at $(2,59)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 23
      },
      {
        "id": "p45-q5-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "Sketch the graph of the function, showing all important features. You do not need to find the $x$-intercepts.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Stationary inflection at $(0,11)$, inflection at $(2,59)$ and maximum at $(3,92)$; both ends tend to $-\\infty$.",
          "worked": "The domain is $\\mathbb R$. The curve increases for $x<3$, with a horizontal tangent at $(0,11)$, and decreases for $x>3$. It is concave down for $x<0$, concave up for $0<x<2$, and concave down for $x>2$. Since the leading term is $-3x^4$, $y\\to-\\infty$ as $x\\to\\pm\\infty$. Mark $(0,11)$, $(2,59)$ and $(3,92)$, and draw the two unlabelled $x$-axis crossings.",
